# Copy to apps/api/.env. Local values come from `pnpm db:start`; Stripe's come from your
# dashboard and from `stripe listen`.
#
# All four must be set or the webhook returns 404 — a deployment with no processor has no
# callback to serve.

SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The project's JWT signing secret. NOT the service-role key.
#
# It signs a 60-second token for the `payment_webhook` Postgres role, which holds no grant on
# any table and exactly one execute (constraints.md D26). A service-role key would discard RLS
# entirely, which D14 refuses. `pnpm db:start` prints this; locally it is the standard
# Supabase development secret.
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# From `stripe listen` (starts `whsec_`). Different per listener — the dashboard's endpoint
# secret and the CLI's are not interchangeable.
#
# This is GOLD's own platform endpoint secret and stays here permanently. A tenant's processor
# account is not configured through the environment: they authorize GOLD from their own Stripe
# account and what gets stored is an `acct_` id in a row, which is not a credential (D27).
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# --- The automation tick -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Separate from the block above: a deployment that takes cards and runs no automations, or the
# other way round, is an ordinary deployment. These four turn the tick on; without them
# /tasks/automation-tick answers 404.
#
# SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_ANON_KEY and SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET above are shared. The JWT secret signs
# a 60-second token for the `automation_runner` role, which holds no grant on any table and
# three executes.

# What the host's scheduler presents on every invocation. Without it the tick is reachable by
# anybody who knows the path — which is a stranger able to make this platform send a business's
# messages. Any long random string; the same value goes in the hosting project's settings.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Channels ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Each channel is configured on its own, and a channel with nothing here is one this deployment
# cannot send on. That is not hidden: a message queued for it is recorded as failed with a
# sentence saying so, which an operator reads on the Automations screen (constraints.md D31).
#
# The sending identity is this deployment's, not a tenant's. A per-tenant sending domain is a
# real feature and a different one; until then the business's own name is in the words it wrote.

RESEND_API_KEY=
MESSAGE_FROM_ADDRESS=

TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
MESSAGE_FROM_NUMBER=

# --- Answering questions ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# What /ai/ask needs, and it is deliberately the shortest block here: SUPABASE_URL and
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY above, plus a model. **No JWT secret.** The other two features mint a token
# for a machine role; this one forwards the access token of the person who asked, so every read
# it performs lands inside their own RLS boundary and there is nothing to sign (constraints.md
# D34). A deployment that can answer questions therefore holds strictly less than one that can
# send a message.
#
# With no model configured the endpoint answers 503 with a sentence, and the Ask screen shows
# that sentence rather than a box that cannot work (D31).

# The default. GOLD's own key, like the drafting one in apps/web.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Or answer with something else that speaks chat completions — a model on your own hardware
# (Ollama, vLLM, text-generation-inference) or a hosted endpoint of that shape. Set, this wins
# over the default, which is usually the point: a deployment whose rows must not leave the
# building sets it and nothing above this line changes.
#
#   ASSISTANT_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
#   ASSISTANT_MODEL=qwen3.5:9b
#
# Both are required together — there is no default model name, because one that resolves to a
# tag you do not have makes a deployment look configured and answer 404 on every question. Give
# the tag exactly as the server lists it (`ollama list`).
#
# ASSISTANT_API_KEY is optional and only for a hosted endpoint that wants one.
#
# **A hosted endpoint that speaks this dialect needs no new code either**, which is the whole
# point of naming this adapter for the dialect rather than for a vendor. Gemini publishes an
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint, so it is three settings and nothing else:
#
#   ASSISTANT_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai
#   ASSISTANT_MODEL=gemini-3.8-flash
#   ASSISTANT_API_KEY=...
#
# Worth knowing before pointing it at a customer's data: a free tier is usually free because the
# provider may train on what you send, and what a tool result contains is a business's own
# customers and jobs. For testing, that is a decision somebody can take knowingly; for a tenant
# who has asked where their rows go, a model on your own hardware is the answer (D34).
ASSISTANT_BASE_URL=
ASSISTANT_MODEL=
ASSISTANT_API_KEY=

# Whether this deployment asks anybody else's service what the weather is doing (D11).
#
# An explicit switch rather than a key check, because unlike every other capability here there
# is no credential whose absence could decide it — the provider is free and asks for nothing. So
# reaching out on a schedule is a decision an operator makes rather than a default they discover.
# Unset means no readings are fetched, and a business with a weather rule is told so.
CONDITIONS_ENABLED=

# The port the local development server listens on. Unset means 8787.
#
# Read only by `dev.py`, which is not what runs in production: on Vercel each route is a function
# and nothing binds a port. It is here so the file describes the whole of what the code reads.
PORT=
